// Runtime/CpuParallelRuntime.maxon — the CPU-parallel QUERIES: `__Builtins.cpuCount()`,
// `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()` and `__Builtins.schedProcessorCount()`.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT THEY SERVE — THE MULTI-CORE VALIDATION HARNESS, AND NOTHING ELSE IN THE LANGUAGE.**
// `maxon-shv2/track0/alloc-torture.maxon` prints both so `validate.sh` can sweep the scheduler's core-count
// clamp (`cpucount=` gives it ncpu) and see that more than one core actually ran the work (`workers=`).
// None has a `stdlib/` wrapper, for `__Builtins.currentProcessId`'s reason: a declaration whose whole
// body is one intrinsic call is the thin wrapper the house rules forbid, and neither reference compiler
// declares one. The bootstrap declares `cpuCount` and `schedMaxActiveWorkers` with these arities and result
// types (`2-Parser.cs:12281-12296`), so THEIR surface is the reference's and only the implementation
// differs; `schedProcessorCount` is shv2's own and says so at its declaration.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THEY ARE THREE QUESTIONS IN TWO BANDS, AND THE SPLIT IS THE FILE'S THESIS.** One asks the MACHINE
// how parallel a run COULD be; the other two ask THIS COMPILER'S SCHEDULER how parallel it WAS CONFIGURED to
// be and how parallel it actually GOT. That difference decides the only thing a `__` band actually
// decides — *may this target run it* — and the two answers are opposite:
//
//   • `__cpu_count` is an OS call, so it lowers only where a backend has landed the read — x64-windows and,
//     since MAC1, arm64-macOS — and must be refused at the call's own span on every other target (E3104, by
//     the `__cpu_` PREFIX, so an entry point added to this band later is gated by construction rather than
//     by memory). ⚠ WHICH targets is `TargetFacilities.targetProvidesFacility`'s `cpuCount` row and is
//     deliberately not counted here: this sentence read "x64-windows and NOWHERE else" for one rung past the
//     day that stopped being true, which is the second copy of a fact earning its keep as a lie.
//   • `__sched_max_active_workers` and `__sched_processor_count` reach no OS at all: each body is one `.data`
//     load and a `ret`, which lowers on every target shv2 emits. Refusing either would be refusing a load.
//     (The first WAS a constant return until G1 gave the mark a writer; the argument is unchanged, because a
//     `.data` load reaches no more OS than a constant does.)
//
// ⇒ **ONE PREFIX CANNOT CARRY BOTH ANSWERS**, because the gate is a prefix test — which is exactly why
// this file wears two `__` bands rather than the one its single subject would suggest. `ProcessRuntime`'s
// two entries share a prefix precisely because they agree on that question and differ only in COST (which
// is what its two `RuntimeUsage` bits are for); these are alike in cost — a handful of instructions
// apiece, no buffer, no allocation, no scheduler — and differ on the target answer. Same file, opposite
// reason.
//
// ⭐ **WHY NOT `GtRuntime`, WHICH OWNS THE SCHEDULER THE OTHER TWO ARE ABOUT.** `ClockRuntime`'s argument,
// verbatim: `isGtRuntimeCallee` is a `__gt_` PREFIX test and it sets `usesGt`, so filing
// `__sched_max_active_workers` there would install the entire green-thread scheduler, the timer store and
// the netpoll for a program whose only question about concurrency is how much of it happened. A program
// may legitimately ask that having spawned nothing — the bootstrap answers 1 for exactly such a program
// (MEASURED) — so the query must not cost a scheduler.
//
// ⚠ **THAT ARGUMENT IS WHY `__sched_processor_count` IS HERE AND NOT `__sched_steal_count`'s SHAPE.** The
// steal count sets `usesGt` outright, because summing `__sched_procs` needs an array only
// `__sched_init_procs` builds; a processor count needs one aligned word, so it rides its own bit and lays
// out that word alone (`SchedRuntime.schedRuntimeGlobals`'s third gate). Asking how many processors a
// scheduler resolved must not be what CREATES one.

// The `__cpu_` prefix — the "does this program ask the MACHINE about itself?" test, and the one
// `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` reads for E3104. One entry point today; a prefix rather than a
// name compare for `isProcessRuntimeCallee`'s stated reason, and that promise has already been tested once
// in this tree: `__proc_pid` arrived after its predicate was written and needed no edit there.
let CpuRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__cpu_"

// ⚠ It is the BOOTSTRAP's spelling for this family (`__sched_active_workers`,
// `__sched_max_active_workers`, `__sched_worker_loop`), taken so that the multi-M port grafts onto the name
// it already has rather than onto one invented here — and since G1 the band really does hold that family:
// `SchedRuntime.maxon` adds `__sched_init_procs`, `__sched_worker_loop`, `__sched_wake_or_spawn` and
// `__sched_drain_pending` beside this file's two queries.

// ⭐ **THE THREE ENTRY POINTS.**
export let CpuCountName = b"__cpu_count"
export let SchedMaxActiveWorkersName = b"__sched_max_active_workers"

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_processor_count()` — HOW MANY Ps THE SCHEDULER RESOLVED, which is the ONE thing
// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` actually decides.** Its band-mate above reads how parallel the run TURNED OUT; this reads
// what the run was CONFIGURED for, and the difference is that the second is not a race. `emitResolveMaxProcs`
// settles `__sched_num_procs` to `osCpuCount`, or to `min(requested, osCpuCount)` when the variable names a
// count, in `__sched_init_procs` — before any green thread runs and without consulting the workload, so two
// runs of one program on one machine cannot disagree.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER REFERENCE DECLARES IT**, exactly as neither declares `__Builtins.schedStealCount` — shv2's
// P/M split is shv2's own (the bootstrap resolves its processor count but exposes no reading of it, and v1
// does not build), so the surface is invented here on the argumentless-integer shape the family already has.
export let SchedProcessorCountName = b"__sched_processor_count"

// ⚠ **THE CLAMP FLOOR IS A CONSTANT AND THE WORKER COUNT IS NOT — that asymmetry is the point of the pair.**
// The floor is a permanent fact about what a processor COUNT can be; the worker high-water mark is a READING
// of this runtime, and since G1 it is a `.data` word two sites maintain.

// The clamp floor a PROCESSOR COUNT never answers below. A real guard over a value that can be 0
// (`GetActiveProcessorCount` on failure) or negative (`sysconf`), and the same guard both bootstrap
// emitters spell in their own instruction set — a count is a divisor for callers, so a 0 must never escape.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED because `SchedRuntime` reads the same RAW op and owes the same floor.** It clamps
// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` against the machine and would otherwise size a `P*` array to zero — see
// `emitResolveMaxProcs`, which records the wild write that costs. Two clamps over one op, from one number:
// a second literal `1` there would be the guard's value written down twice.
export let MinimumProcessorCount = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE ASK THE MACHINE FOR ITS PROCESSOR COUNT?** The `__cpu_`-only test, the exact twin of
// `isClockRuntimeCallee`. Deliberately NARROW in the same way: it implies no scheduler, no heap and no leak
// gate, because the body it installs is one import call and a compare.
export function isCpuCountRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: CpuRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isCpuCountRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE ASK THE SCHEDULER HOW MANY WORKER Ms IT HAS RUN?**
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS A NAME TEST AND IT USED TO BE A `__sched_` PREFIX TEST, WHICH G1 FALSIFIED.** The prefix was
// chosen when the band held exactly this one entry, under a comment saying so; `SchedRuntime.maxon` then put
// four scheduler INTERNALS in the same band, and a prefix test cannot tell a QUERY a program asked from
// machinery the compiler installed. Nothing broke — the internals are named only by builder-built Std, which
// `recordCallUsage` never scans — so this is the enumerated-door shape that fails OPEN: the day one of them
// became reachable from a Maxon-tier op it would have silently set "this program asks how parallel it was"
// and laid out a `.data` word for a question nobody asked. The name is what the bit is about.
//
// ⚠ It is NOT in `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, and that absence is the decision this file exists to make
// rather than an omission — see the header.
//
// ⚠ **ITS BAND-MATE `__sched_processor_count` IS ASKED FOR SEPARATELY AND NOT THROUGH HERE**, because the two
// answer to different `.data` words and therefore to different bits: one name test that set both would lay
// out a word for a question the program did not ask, which is the whole discipline `RuntimeUsage` keeps.
export function isSchedRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(SchedMaxActiveWorkersName)
end 'isSchedRuntimeCallee'

// ⚠ **ONE GUARD PER ENTRY, NOT ONE FOR THE FILE**, on `installProcessRuntime`'s terms: these are
// independent questions and a program almost always wants one of them. Here the saving is smaller than
// that file's (no entry drags in the heap), but the discipline is the same and the cost of getting it
// wrong is an import — or a `.data` word — a program never uses.
export function installCpuParallelRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesCpuCount 'cpuCount'
		buildCpuCount(module)
	end 'cpuCount'

	if usage.usesSchedMaxActiveWorkers 'schedMaxActiveWorkers'
		buildSchedWordQuery(module, name: SchedMaxActiveWorkersName, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel)
	end 'schedMaxActiveWorkers'

	if usage.usesSchedProcessorCount 'schedProcessorCount'
		buildSchedWordQuery(module, name: SchedProcessorCountName, label: SchedNumProcsLabel)
	end 'schedProcessorCount'
end 'installCpuParallelRuntime'

// ⭐⭐ **`__cpu_count() -> i64`** — the number of logical processors the host reports, never below 1.
//
// The op asks the question and the CLAMP is done here, in builder-built Std, for the reason
// `StdOp.osCpuCount`'s own comment states: the two platform answers fail differently (`0` from
// `GetActiveProcessorCount`, `-1` from `sysconf`) and one SIGNED `< 1` test covers both, so a future POSIX
// lane supplies only the read. Both bootstrap emitters spell the same guard in their own instruction set —
// x86 `test`/`jnz` (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:8076`) and arm64 `cmp #1`/`b.ge`
// (`ARM64CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:115`) — and both say why: the count is a divisor for callers, so a 0 must
// never escape.
//
// ⚠ **THE GUARD IS NOT DEAD CODE, AND IT WAS MEASURED REACHED.** `GetActiveProcessorCount` returns 0 when the
// group number is invalid, and `ALL_PROCESSOR_GROUPS` never is — so on x64-windows this arm is unreachable
// through this call site, exactly as `__proc_exe_path`'s unavailable arm is. Sabotaged to an INVALID group
// number, though, the API really does answer 0 and this arm really does convert it: the spec's bounds cases
// stayed green under that sabotage and went red the moment the guard was also removed. It is here because
// "the OS cannot fail" is not something a runtime gets to assume, and because it is the arm a POSIX read
// WILL reach.
function buildCpuCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CpuCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var clamped = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpufloor")
	var reported = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpuok")

	let raw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osCpuCount(raw))

	// ⚠ `clamped` and `reported` carry NO block args and take no edge arguments: `entry` dominates both, so
	// `raw` is simply in scope in the arm that answers it — the dominance argument `__proc_exe_path`'s four exit
	// blocks state for themselves.
	let floor = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	let belowFloor = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: raw, b: floor, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowFloor, thenBlockId: clamped.id, elseBlockId: reported.id))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: clamped, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(reported, op: StdOp.ret(raw))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCpuCount'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SCHEDULER QUERIES, FROM ONE BODY: `name() -> i64` answers the `.data` word at `label`.**
//
// `__sched_max_active_workers` reads the high-water mark of concurrently-active green-thread worker Ms;
// `__sched_processor_count` reads how many Ps `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` resolved to. Both are a `globalAddr` and a
// load, and a second hand-built body would be that one fact written down twice — the shape this codebase
// keeps finding. What legitimately differs between them is the WORD, which is the parameter, and the GATE,
// which is the caller's.
//
// ⚠ **THE PAIR SHARES A BODY BUT NOT A SEED, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE.** The worker mark is seeded
// to 1 because a program with no scheduler really does run on one M — its own; the processor count is seeded
// to 0 because a scheduler that has not initialized has built no P, which is also what
// `__sched_steal_count` and `__sched_any_executing` read that word for. Both readings are the truth about
// the moment they are taken, and neither costs an instruction to arrange (`SchedRuntime.schedRuntimeGlobals`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_max_active_workers` WAS A `ret 1`, AND THAT WAS THE HONEST SPELLING RIGHT UP TO THE RUNG
// THAT GAVE IT A WRITER.** The old body's argument was that a global with no writer is a constant wearing a
// variable's clothes: it costs a `.data` word and a load, tells the next reader that something maintains it,
// and reads 0 rather than 1 in any program whose scheduler never installs. Every clause of that was true
// while shv2 had no worker M. G1 built one, `__sched_worker_loop` raises the mark on each entry, and the two objections that remained are
// both answered where they arise rather than here: the slot is SEEDED TO 1 in `.data`
// (`SchedRuntime.schedRuntimeGlobals`), so a program with no scheduler reads the truth with no code at all,
// and it is laid out on `usesSchedMaxActiveWorkers` as well as on `usesGt`, so asking the question does not
// require having spawned anything.
//
// ⚠ **THE IOCP COMPLETION THREAD IS STILL NOT A WORKER M, AND IT IS THE ONE THING THAT COULD MAKE THIS LOOK
// WRONG.** `__io_init` creates an OS thread, but it drains completions and re-readies parked green threads —
// it never RUNS one, and it never adopts a P. The count is raised in `__sched_worker_loop` and nowhere else,
// which is exactly where the bootstrap raises its own and exactly where it declines to count its completion
// thread either.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT STILL ANSWERS 1 FOR AN `async` PROGRAM, THOUGH NO LONGER FOR THE REASON IT USED TO.** The old
// reason was the default: `SchedRuntime.DefaultMaxProcs` was 1, so there was no second P to start an M on.
// That constant is gone and the default is the machine's count — but an `async` frame is a COROUTINE of its
// caller, published only to its owner's queue, so nothing calls `__sched_wake_or_spawn` and no worker M is
// created at ANY count. `specs-shv2/builtins-cpu-parallel.md`'s `sched-max-active-workers-is-one-under-async`
// pins that, and it is what makes the mark a measurement rather than a promise. A `spawn` DOES produce green
// threads and does raise the mark; `track0/pin-matrix.sh` asserts both families.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS EXACTLY WHY THE PROCESSOR COUNT HAD TO EXIST (2026-09-01).** The mark is a reading of what
// the SCHEDULER DID, so a program that finishes its backlog before `__sched_wake_or_spawn` needs a second M
// reads 1 at four processors — legitimately. `specs-shv2/sched-default-procs.md`'s
// `the-procs-marker-raises-the-processor-count` asserted that reading and was therefore green filtered and
// RED under a loaded full suite (12 spec workers over 16 cores). What `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` promises is the P
// COUNT, and that is what a case about the marker must assert; the worker mark stays what it is, a
// measurement of an outcome, and belongs only in cases that can tolerate one.
function buildSchedWordQuery(module StdModule, name ByteArray, label ByteArray)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	let wordAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(wordAddr, label: label))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: wordAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedWordQuery'
